## Attribution audit

The current standard states the following relevant limits:

- R6.1: code 2 is `Usage / parse error`; unlisted failures collapse to 1.
- R8.2: unavailable prompting for missing required non-auth input or consent is
  code 2; missing authentication/authorization input is code 4; runtime input
  failures such as unreadable files are code 1.
- R5.5 and R5.6 constrain secret intake and redaction but assign no exit code.

I checked every current standard-attribution location in `review/DECISION.md`:

| Location | Current claim | Result |
|---|---|---|
| 76-94 | R6.1 taxonomy, R7.7/R7.8, R9.6 | supported; no config-value derivation |
| 122-124 | AC3/AC4 | supported general taxonomy; explicitly P03 proposals |
| 255-276 | configuration-failure narrative | supported: 104=R8.2/1; 202=R6.1/2; 203=template choice |
| 383 | owned parameter rows | 203 and 204 explicitly template choices; 205 is R8.2 missing input |
| 464 | E10 evidence-row summary | reports the standard's 2/4/1 split only |
| 575-603 | Disagreement 4 basis table and reasoning | all five rows classify to the correct source or choice |
| 605-619 | closed-stdin correction | supported by R8.2 and R6.1 SIGINT definition |
| 724-804 | revision history | accurately describes the superseded error and revision-4 repair |

The raw reports were checked against Disagreement 4. `raw/codex.md` assigns
configuration to 1; `raw/opus.md`, `raw/doxa-openai.normalized.md`, and
`raw/doxa-perplexity.normalized.md` assign configuration to 2; and
`raw/doxa-gemini.normalized.md` gives an internally inconsistent I/O mapping.
The decision records that split as engine input rather than standard authority.

